WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.719 --> 00:00:24.550
When the year 2020 came to an end, the world
had high expectations for 2021.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:32.540
2020 was a year lost to COVID-19, with many
people spending the majority of the year in

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.920
tedious lockdowns and in fear.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:42.080
So 2021 had to be better, right?

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.909
The worst seemed to be behind us.

00:00:45.909 --> 00:00:51.480
Vaccines were about to be delivered, economies
were predicted to quickly recover, and a new

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.470
year was on the horizon.

00:00:53.470 --> 00:01:10.150
But what happened instead was another lurching,
stammering year.

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:16.820
The pandemic has now claimed more than five
million lives worldwide and destroyed tens

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:31.840
of millions more through illness, financial
hardship and grief.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:37.490
Just when one COVID-19 strain seemed to be
under control, a new variant emerged in 2021

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:50.170
– and we were suddenly all memorizing Greek
letters, from alpha to omicron.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:56.020
2021 saw a huge divide form between the vaccinated
and unvaccinated.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:02:03.540
While poorer parts of the world are still
waiting for access to vaccines – only 7.6

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:09.649
percent of people in low-income countries
have received at least one jab – richer

00:02:09.649 --> 00:02:15.610
nations have been confronted by a vocal minority
that refuses to take them.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:16.850
Governments had to take action.

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:25.810
Some didn’t give people a choice and introduced
mandates, while others tried encouraging citizens

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:32.310
by restricting where the unvaccinated could
go, barring them from restaurants or even

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:35.069
their workplaces.

00:02:35.069 --> 00:02:42.760
Tougher restrictions triggered massive protests.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:50.490
And while anti-vaxxers hit the streets in
anger, the patience of those who have already

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:57.840
had their booster shots is starting to disappear,
as mental health struggles and substance abuse

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:04.160
continue to rise amid ongoing lockdowns.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:13.640
2021 also saw a number of geopolitical conflicts
across the world.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:21.870
Myanmar's military ousted the government in
a coup that sparked huge protests nationwide

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:26.849
and resulted in a bloody crackdown.

00:03:26.849 --> 00:03:34.349
In South Africa, following the imprisonment
of ex-president Jacob Zuma for contempt of

00:03:34.349 --> 00:03:41.739
court, there was an outbreak of unrest and
looting.

00:03:41.739 --> 00:03:49.430
The year-long conflict between rebels and
government forces in the Tigray region of

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:58.010
northern Ethiopia intensified.

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:05.680
Lebanon was faced with one of the worst economic
crises since the mid-19th century.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:12.370
Shortages in fuel, medication and multiple
power cuts prompted widespread anger.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:20.959
Tension between the US and China remained
unresolved this year, while Russia’s intentions

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:29.350
towards Ukraine raised concerns.

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:35.600
Unrest caused concerns within the US as well.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:49.360
Images of the US Capitol being stormed and
attacked by supporters of Donald Trump shocked

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:50.360
the world.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:57.380
The assassination of Haiti’s president and
a subsequent earthquake caused thousands of

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:03.790
Haitians to flee their country.

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:06.940
And it wasn’t just Haitians on the run.

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:13.530
A surge in migrants crossing the English Channel
from France triggered a diplomatic row between

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:20.270
Paris and London.

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:24.840
When the internationally backed Afghan government
collapsed and the Taliban took control, many

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:30.370
Afghans fled what Amnesty International later
called “repeated war crimes and relentless

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:31.370
bloodshed.”

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:43.060
Meanwhile, Belarus encouraged migrants to
cross its territory to enter Latvia, Lithuania,

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:44.460
and Poland.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:54.669
Critics called it a bid to pressure the EU
to end sanctions it imposed after the rigged

00:05:54.669 --> 00:06:02.590
2020 Belarussian presidential election.

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:09.050
All in all, the European Union saw a 70 percent
increase in the number of people entering

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:22.280
the bloc illegally in 2021 compared to the
previous year.

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:28.910
Some 84 million people around the world have
been forcibly displaced from their homes.

00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:37.100
Conflict, economic collapse, and climate change
are likely to drive that number higher in

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:42.090
the coming years.

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:50.680
2021 was perceived as a make-or-break year
for climate action.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:58.889
The climate emergency became more palpable
as fires, droughts, tornadoes and flooding

00:06:58.889 --> 00:07:10.120
scarred more of the planet.

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:18.930
At the beginning of the year, Australia was
overwhelmed by floods, which was later mirrored

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:28.760
in Germany, where flash flooding killed more
than 200 people and caused billions of euros

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:33.789
in damage.

00:07:33.789 --> 00:07:42.860
Meanwhile, heat records were topped across
the world.

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:52.281
In Canada, the heat killed more than 230 people,
while 36 tornadoes caused havoc across the

00:07:52.281 --> 00:07:53.450
US.

00:07:53.450 --> 00:08:00.680
What’s more, extreme wildfires were ablaze
around the Mediterranean, in California, Siberia

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:03.160
and the Amazon Forest.

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:12.889
The IPCC published an extensive report based
on more than 14,000 studies warning that climate

00:08:12.889 --> 00:08:19.970
change is more advanced than previously assumed
and is definitely caused by humans.

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:29.400
But there was also some positive news about
the the climate in 2021.

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:35.630
China promised not to build any new coal power
stations abroad.

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:39.900
And the climate conference in Scotland agreed
on funding for poorer nations to green their

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:45.040
economies and adapt to the effects of global
warming.

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:52.199
2021 also saw two unprecedented court cases
in Europe that could have a long-lasting impact.

00:08:52.199 --> 00:08:56.850
Germany's Federal Constitutional Court ruled
that the freedoms and fundamental rights of

00:08:56.850 --> 00:09:03.940
young people have been violated by insufficient
climate protection from the state.

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:08.369
And in the Netherlands, a court ordered gas
company Shell to reduce its worldwide CO2

00:09:08.369 --> 00:09:14.389
emissions by 45% by 2030, compared to 2019
levels.

00:09:14.389 --> 00:09:18.730
Experts believe this will result in a chain
reaction and trigger companies and countries

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:25.790
to take action before they are dragged before
court.

00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:29.579
So in 2021, we were faced with one crisis
after the next.

00:09:29.579 --> 00:09:34.699
We had to grapple with the reality of the
impact of COVID-19, climate change and conflicts

00:09:34.699 --> 00:09:35.720
across the world.

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:42.199
But we also learned that we are more resilient
than we might think, that humanity can rise

00:09:42.199 --> 00:09:45.730
above, and that together we can be better.

